# Sprint Review and Retrospective — Executing
#
# The two events that close an iteration. They are different meetings about
# different things, and running them as one is the usual way both get lost.

screen: review
title: Review and Retrospective
phase: Executing
process: Agile Practice Guide — iteration review and retrospective
order: 74
requires: sprints
teaches: |
  The difference between reviewing the product and reviewing the way you work,
  and why they are separate events. By the end you should be able to say what
  a retrospective produces, and why a retrospective with no action from it is
  a complaints meeting.
before: |
  A sprint that has ended. Both of these are backward-looking: there is nothing
  to review until something was finished or was not.
after: |
  The next sprint's capacity, and one change to how the work is done. A
  retrospective that does not change the next iteration did not happen.
intro: |
  Two events, deliberately not one. The review asks whether the product is
  right: what got built, what the sponsor accepts, what comes back. The
  retrospective asks whether the process is right: what worked, what did not,
  and the one thing being changed next time.

  Keeping them apart matters because they have different audiences and
  different outputs. A review that drifts into process talk wastes the
  stakeholder's time; a retrospective with stakeholders in the room stops being
  honest.
source: |
  Agile Practice Guide, published with the PMBOK Guide 6th ed.: iteration
  review and retrospective. Acceptance of what was built is 5.5 Validate Scope;
  the lessons are 4.4 Manage Project Knowledge, and they end up on the Closeout
  screen.

field reviews
  prompt: For each finished sprint: what was demonstrated, and what was actually accepted?
  check column:accepted: A review with no acceptance decision is a demo. Say what was accepted, what came back, and who decided.
  label: Sprint reviews
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: demonstrated
  tone_column: accepted
  source: |
    Agile Practice Guide: the iteration review, where the increment is shown to
    stakeholders and accepted or not. Formal acceptance is 5.5 Validate Scope.
  why: |
    The point of the review is a decision, not a demonstration. Somebody with
    the authority to accept the work either accepts it or says what is wrong,
    and that answer is what makes the increment real.

    Work that comes back is the most useful row in this table. It is the
    cheapest possible feedback: found at the end of one iteration rather than
    at the end of the project.
  if_blank: |
    Sprints end without anybody saying whether what came out of them was any
    good, and the first real acceptance conversation happens at closeout.
  example: |
    Sprint 1 | Eleven screens generated from definition files | Accepted | Sponsor | Walkthrough asked for, logged as CR5
  column sprint
    label: Sprint
    type: line
    width: 2
  column held_on
    label: Date
    type: date
    width: 2
  column demonstrated
    label: What was demonstrated
    type: text
    width: 5
  column accepted
    label: Outcome
    type: choice
    options: Accepted=ok, Accepted with changes=warn, Rejected=danger, Not reviewed=faint
    width: 2
  column decided_by
    label: Accepted by
    type: line
    width: 2
  column feedback
    label: What came back
    type: text
    width: 5

field retro
  prompt: For each finished sprint: what worked, what did not, and what will you change?
  check column:action: A retrospective entry with no action is a complaint. Say what changes next sprint.
  label: Retrospectives
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: observation
  tone_column: kind
  source: |
    Agile Practice Guide: the iteration retrospective. Its output feeds the
    lessons learned register (4.4 Manage Project Knowledge).
  why: |
    One action per retrospective, not ten. A retrospective that produces a list
    changes nothing, because nobody can hold a list in their head through a
    sprint; one change, actually made, compounds.

    The honest ones are specific. "Communication was poor" is not a finding;
    "the reviewer got the charter after the build started, so his first
    feedback was about scope" is, and you can do something about it.
  if_blank: |
    The same problem recurs every sprint and each time it feels like bad luck.
  example: |
    Sprint 1 | Scope changed twice mid-sprint | What to change | Log a change request the moment scope moves, before building | PM
  column sprint
    label: Sprint
    type: line
    width: 2
  column observation
    label: What happened
    type: text
    width: 5
  column kind
    label: Type
    type: choice
    options: What worked=ok, What to change=warn, Surprise=accent
    width: 2
  column action
    label: What changes next sprint
    type: text
    width: 5
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column done
    label: Carried out
    type: choice
    options: Yes=ok, Not yet=warn, Dropped=danger
    width: 1
